index 4b5b2061cf9aeb7b09da0f7f3ed283ca7e6bda08..4d760c578188739501455f61bb911cb15ca779b4 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -23,3 +23,13 @@ with them. Operations apply in order; it throws an `Error` if the patch is malfo
 index, failed `test`, moving a value into its own child, removing the whole document). The patch is atomic:
 on error no result is returned. Paths are JSON Pointers, parsed as in the section above; `add` accepts `-`
 to append to an array, and `test` compares values by JSON equality (object member order is irrelevant).
+
+## JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386)
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, applies a JSON Merge Patch to a parsed JSON
+document and returns the merged document. An object patch is merged member by member: a `null` member
+removes that member from the target, any other member is set (objects merge recursively, arrays and
+primitives replace). A patch that is not an object replaces the whole target, and a target that is not an
+object is treated as empty when an object patch is applied. Since `null` always means removal, a merge patch
+cannot set a member to `null`. The inputs are never mutated and the result shares no structure with them; it
+throws an `Error` if the patch is not a JSON value (`undefined`, `NaN`, functions, class instances, ...).
